---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="04-functions"
  lessonSlug="01-clamp"
  hook="The smallest useful function with multiple parameters. Every UI slider, audio mixer, and game has this."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Return <code>value</code> confined to <code>[lo, hi]</code>. Below
    range → return <code>lo</code>. Above → return <code>hi</code>.
    Inside → return <code>value</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The pattern</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def clamp(value, lo, hi):
    if value < lo:
        return lo
    elif value > hi:
        return hi
    else:
        return value

for v in [5, -3, 99, 0, 10, -5]:
    print(f"clamp({v:>3}, 0, 10) = {clamp(v, 0, 10)}")`}
  />

  <h2>Early-return form (also fine)</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="return exits the function — no else needed"
    code={`def clamp(value, lo, hi):
    if value < lo:
        return lo
    if value > hi:
        return hi
    return value         # only reached if neither earlier return fired

print(clamp(5, 0, 10), clamp(-3, 0, 10), clamp(99, 0, 10))`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Forgetting a branch">
    <p>
      A common bug: forget the final <code>return value</code>. For inputs
      inside the range, both <code>if</code>s are false, the function falls
      off the end and returns <code>None</code>. Python won't warn you.
      The test <code>test_inside</code> is the one that catches it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Quiz
    question="clamp(5, 0, 10) — what does this return?"
    choices={[
      { text: "0",   explain: "0 is only returned when value < lo. 5 is not less than 0." },
      { text: "10",  explain: "10 is only returned when value > hi. 5 is not greater than 10." },
      { text: "5",   correct: true, explain: "Right. 5 is inside [0, 10], so the function returns value unchanged." },
      { text: "None", explain: "Only if you forgot the else branch! That's a common bug — the test test_inside catches it." },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>01-clamp.py</code>. 6 tests including both boundaries and a
    negative-range case.
  </p>
</Lecture>
